{
  "instructions": "- Do not show IDs to the user; use names instead.\\n- Never invent UUIDs.\\n- When there is no exact task-title match, use a result only when its meaning clearly matches the request.\\n- A synonym for task is ticket.\\n- Do not set time-booking fields the user did not specify.\\n- Before creating a project task from a project name, call get-projects and use the exact project ID; ask the user when multiple projects are plausible.\\n- Before editing a task, call get-tasks to resolve its exact UUID and project. Pass only fields the user asked to change so all other task details remain unchanged.\\n- Set isPrivate=true only when the user explicitly requests a private task.\\n- Resolve task statuses with get-task-statuses, parent tasks with get-tasks scoped to the project, task lists with get-task-lists, assignees with get-project-members, and types of work with get-types-of-work.\\n- Only use a task without parentId as a parent task; awork tasks support one subtask level.\\n- When creating a subtask and no type of work was requested, use the parent task's typeOfWorkId.\\n- For assigneeIds, join the returned userId values as a comma-separated string.\\n- Omit taskStatusId and typeOfWorkId when creating a task and the user did not request a specific value so awork applies its defaults.\\n- If a lookup tool reports a permission error, explain the required project permission and do not describe it as an empty result.\\n- Treat create-task warnings as a successfully created task with a partial follow-up failure. Include returned task URLs when available.",
